  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nSPC has drawn up a risk level 1 out of 5 (marginal) for strong storms\nmid to late evening. The 12Z CAMs are in general agreement that storms\nare expected to develop off the Raton Mesa and could impact far southwest\nKansas after 6 pm tonight. The main impact/threat would be strong\noutflow winds of 50 to 60 mph, since the environment will be hot\nand mixed with plenty of DCAPE forecast. Again, it looks like that\nfar southwest Kansas stands to see the highest chance for storm activity\ntonight. The rest of the FA is likely to remain on the dry side.\nOtherwise, a weak boundary will move across the state late tonight\nand into the morning hours. The main impact will be wind shift with\nnortherly gusty winds in the wake of the front. Otherwise, tonight\nwill see lows mainly in the 60s.\n\nThe second impact from the passage of the weak boundary will be a\nslight cool down in highs. Actually, highs will be fairly close to\nnormal values with highs peaking in the lower 90s. Lows once again\nin the 60s are expected heading into Sunday morning. No precipitation\nis expected other than the storms for Thursday evening.\n\nThe mid level anticyclone will move from the southwest part of the\nUnited States to over the Rockies and plains by Wednesday and Thursday.\nThis supports the notion of a dry forecast with increasing highs\nthrough the business week. Highs should peak into the upper 90s to\nlower 10s as we close out the business week. Lows will also trend\nwarmer with more 70s instead of 60s, as we finish out the rest of\nthe period.",
